{
  "name": "416. Kling video on the contract: create by model path, query by task id or client_reference_id, Units as vendor_cost, refusals (kling video contract, provider error fixture)",
  "description": "Pins the Kling provider (TXE-6430) against contract C3, C4, C5, C6, C7 and C9 on the unified video routes. Rows 1 to 7 and 15 to 19 are a local fixture regression with no live provider calls: start tests/e2e/api/runners/provider-error-fixture.mjs, start an isolated gateway on tests/e2e/api/provider_config/provider-error-fixture.config.json and run with providerErrorFixture=1; they are skipped otherwise. In that config the built-in kling provider points at the fixture with two keys, kling-key-a and kling-key-b. The fixture answers Kling's new design standard API (kling.ai/document-api): POST /text-to-video/{model}, /image-to-video/{model} and /omni-video/{model}, GET /tasks?task_ids= and ?external_task_ids=, POST /tasks. It keeps every request with its key and body under /__kling-calls and counts it under 'kling <METHOD> <path> <bearer token>'. Rows 2 to 4: a create picks the path by model and input (text-to-video without a first frame, image-to-video with one), sends seconds, resolution, aspect_ratio and audio unchanged in settings, the caller's client_reference_id as options.external_task_id and callback_url as options.callback_url, the extra field watermark as options.watermark_info and every other extra field in settings, and returns the task object. Rows 5 and 6: a query by Kling's task id and a query by the caller's client_reference_id (GET /v1/videos/{client_reference_id}:kling?id_type=client_reference_id) return the same task object with provider_status, videos[].url, expires_at = completion + 30 days, usage.output_seconds and Kling's Units as usage.vendor_cost{unit: unit}. Row 7 is the list, Kling's query by cursor. Rows 8 to 14 need no upstream and run wherever a kling provider is configured: seconds, resolution and aspect_ratio outside the model's tiers, seed, expires_after_seconds and output_storage_uri are each 400 caller_unsupported_parameter naming the field, and the cancel route is 400 provider_cancel_unsupported, because Kling has no cancel API. Row 15: Kling's content security block of a create (HTTP 400, service code 1301) is caller_content_filtered with error.code 1301. Row 16: a task Kling's risk control failed is status failed with content_filter. Row 17: a client_reference_id Kling has no task for is 404 task_not_found. Row 18: a provider whose vendor cannot be queried by the caller's id refuses id_type=client_reference_id with 400 caller_unsupported_parameter. Row 19 reads the fixture's counters: each create and each query was exactly one upstream call with the key x-bf-api-key named, and the eight refused requests made none. Rows 20 to 22 are live and [PREVIEW] (INCLUDE_PREVIEW=1), because a Kling task costs Units: create a 5 second 720p kling-2.5-turbo task with a client_reference_id, poll it to completion, and find it again by that client_reference_id. They need KLING_API_KEY in the gateway's environment. Before the change none of this existed: the fork had no kling provider (POST /v1/videos with a kling/ model answered an unknown-provider error) and GET /v1/videos/{id} had no id_type form.",
  "item": [
    {
